abstracts.content:
The office of vicar general in Spirituals (in spiritualibus) was of key importance in managing dioceses in late medieval Poland. In contrast to the strictly judicial function of an episcopal official (judicial vicar), the role of a vicar general was to deputize for his bishop in administrative functions – filling the numerous ecclesiastical benefices (accepting resignations, approving replacements, granting possession), and to maintain clerical discipline (under the sanction of suspension or removal from a benefice). As these tasks bore certain similarities, in some bishoprics in Poland (and beyond), the offices of vicar general and official were entrusted to the same person. This custom had already become established during the first half of the fifteenth century in the Archdiocese of Gniezno. Despite this, in the first days of his pontificate Archbishop Władysław Oporowski of Gniezno (1449-1453) appointed a separate vicar general, namely Sędek z Czechla (often referred to by historians as Sędziwój). He held this office until the death of the ordinary. This experiment was continued by Archbishop Jan Sprowski (1453-1464), who initially appointed Jan z Brzóstkowa (1454-1456) as vicar. The activities of these two vicars, Sędek z Czechla and, to a lesser extent, Jan z Brzóstkowa, are documented in detail in the vicarial register preserved to this day in several parts, with entries from 1449-1453 and 1455. A detailed discussion and a critical edition of these records are the subject of this work.
The first part of the book is a critical source study of the vicarial register. First, the state of research and editorial work on late medieval ecclesiastical court registers which is relatively modest, is discussed. Western European scholars have published editions of the records of particular judicial vicariates (including documentation from Augsburg, Brussels, Cambrai, Cerisy-la-Forêt, Lausanne, Marseilles and Aix-en-Provence, Paris, Prague, and Tournai). Only the records of ad hoc inquisition tribunals and ecclesiastical courts of various ranks from the territory of Great Britain have attracted more interest. In Poland, however, there are only selections of the more interesting records, prepared according to subjective criteria by Bolesław Ulanowski (especially Acta capitulorum nec non iudiciorum ecclesiasticorum selecta, vol. 2, Cracow 1902, vol. 3, part 1, Cracow 1908) and the edition of the entire book of the Lviv Consistory from 1482-1498, prepared by Wilhelm Rolny (Acta officii consistorialis Leopoliensis antiquissima, 2 vols, Lviv 1927-1930).
In view of the multitude of volumes of books and their considerable research value, the small number of ecclesiastical court records published to date provides good justification for renewing efforts to make this type of documentation available to a wider audience. The subsequent part of the first chapter outlines the reasons for choosing the court register kept by Sędek z Czechla and Jan z Brzóstkowa as the subject of this study and edition: 1) this manuscript is the only one from the fifteenth-century (arch-)diocese of Gniezno that documents exclusively the activities of vicars general in Spirituals; 2) the cases entrusted to Sędek z Czechla’s judgment – matters of clerical discipline and morality, breaches of ecclesiastical immunity, sacrilege, heresy and public sins, and various types of disputes described as ‘matrimonial’ (causae matrimoniales) – are also extremely interesting to modern readers; 3) the register contains extensive records of witness testimony – a dozen or so pages of Sędek’s book, unfilled by his scribes in 1449-1453, were used in 1455 to record the testimony of witnesses questioned by his successor as vicar general of Gniezno, Jan z Brzóstkowa, and the statements recorded here concern mainly matrimonial matters and clerical offences; 4) the lack of an established practice of keeping separate vicarial records, the relatively small number of incoming cases and the frequent use of a simplified oral procedure resulted in a more accurate and extensive record of the parties’ claims than in the case of records kept by the bishop’s official (the records focus on describing facts rather than procedural and legal issues); 5) of the 826 entries printed in the present work, Ulanowski (op. cit.) and Adolf Kunkel (‘Gnesener Hussitenverhöre 1450-1452’, Mitteilungen des Instituts für Österreichische Geschichtsforschung 38, 1920, no. 2, pp. 314-325) published only 44.
The second chapter of the critical study is devoted to a detailed codicological description of the editorial basis of the edition. As I have attempted to demonstrate here, in its historical form, the vicarial register consisted of as many as 3 now extant archival units: 1) a manuscript from the Archdiocesan Archive in Gniezno, Acta Causarum Consistorii Gnesnensis, shelf mark ACons. A 30 (in folio format, 105 folios); 2) an unregistered parchment binding from the collection of the Archdiocesan Archive in Gniezno (removed from the manuscript ACons. A 30 for unknown reasons during conservation in the 1980s); 3) a collection of Faith Case Records (Acta causarum fidei) held in the State Archive in Poznań in the Ecclesiastical Documents and Records Holding, shelf mark 414 (4 folios most probably extracted between 1876 and 1886 from ACons. A 30). This chapter also discusses the extraordinary care exercised in keeping the register. The part documenting Sędek z Czechla’s activities was undoubtedly a clean copy, transcribed by two or three ‘main’ scribes (in addition to which there were a dozen or so ‘occasional’ scribes; it was also possible to distinguish handwritten notes on the announcement of sentences made by Sędek himself). The innovative nature of this record lay in the fact that the entries in the register were arranged chronologically, but according to subject – the manuscript was divided into 3 sections containing records of cases relating to the clerical estate and public sins (here the entries from 1449-1453), records of matrimonial cases (1450-1452) and records of matters of faith (1450-1452). The fragment with the testimony of witnesses given before the court of Jan z Brzóskowa, on the other hand, was inscribed in 1455 in the empty space between the first and second parts of Sędek’s records. This has the nature of a rough-copy record, kept by a hasty and careless, yet also expert scribe. 
The third and final chapter of the source study presents the basic facts of the biographies of the vicars general (education, family relations, ecclesiastical career), especially those that may have been relevant to the way in which they held office. In the case of Sędek z Czechla, who bore the Korab coat of arms, and was a well-known historian, royal advisor and canon of Gniezno between 1432 and 1458 (later a canon regular at Kłodawa from 1458 until his death in 1476), his participation in Church councils (Ferrara, Basel) and his theological, rather than legal training (several years of divinity studies in Paris) were of particular importance. This was the reason, as discussed with specific examples in this study, that Sędek treated delinquent clergy and laymen in a rather paternal fashion. His intention was not to punish or enforce the norms of synodal The second, and most comprehensive, element of the book is a critical edition of 826 entries from the vicarial register. Part I consists of records of cases concerning the clerical estate and public sins (clerical disciplinary offences, sacrilege, violations of ecclesiastical immunity, generally reproachable acts on the part of the laity, Nos 1-500). Part II – Matrimonial cases (including disputes relating to concubinage, prostitution and love magic, Nos 501-739); Part III – Files of cases of faith (mainly anti-Hussite trials, Nos 740-757); Part IV – Testimony of witnesses (Nos 758-822). The edition is accompanied by Appendices, which include the rates of court fees recorded on the parchment binding and the 1455 court proceedings added to the ACons A 30 book (Nos 823-826). The text of the records was prepared for publication according to a slightly modified version of the instructions outlined in: Adam Wolff (‘Projekt instrukcji wydawniczej dla pisanych źródeł historycznych do połowy XVI wieku’, Studia Źródłoznawcze, 1, 1957, pp. 159-181). The source text is therefore essentially transliterated (with certain described exceptions), and punctuation and the use of upper- and lower-case letters follow modern practice.
In editing the Latin text, the following editorial symbols have been used:
|abc| – deletion of a word or text passage;
|=abc=| – crossing out (deleting) an entire entry;
<abc> – text added between the lines or in the margin;
(abc) – addition of letters mistakenly omitted by the scribe, expansion of an ambiguous
abbreviation;
[?] – uncertain reading of a word;
[??] – uncertain reading of a textual fragment;
[!] – confirmation of reading of erroneous or peculiar recording;
[!!] – confirmation of the reading of a grammatically incorrect sentence in the text;
[.......] – illegible word(s) or a lacuna in the text, usually the result of physical damage to the manuscript (see Chapter II for a description of the manuscript’s state of preservation); the number of dots indicates the approximate number of missing letters; 
[abc] – lacuna text reconstructed according to the editor’s conjecture.
In addition, italicized foliation, and explanation of dates mentioned in the text appear in the text in square brackets. Individual entries have been given sequential numbered headings in the edition, followed by italicized summaries in Polish. Beneath the Latin text of the entries, where necessary, cross-references are provided to link the entry in question to other records concerning the same matter (abbreviation ‘zob.’ [see]), or to draw attention to clearly related entries (abbreviation ‘por.’ [cf.]). Since a small number of the notes printed here have already been published, references to earlier publications have been included in the critical apparatus (abbreviated ‘wyd.’ [ed.]). The apparatus also includes textual footnotes with various kinds of critical comments on the appearance of the manuscript and the wording of the source text. Differences between the reading of the source text and the reading proposed by previous editors are also indicated here. Factual footnotes are replaced for the most part by the index of persons and places (which should be consulted for the identification and location of places). For this reason, only a few notes identifying legal texts cited in the source can be found under the textual record; other footnotes report perceived inconsistencies with the lists of officials published in the series Urzędnicy Dawnej Rzeczypospolitej XII–XVIII Wieku. Spisy and errors in the dating of records. In a few cases (Nos 721, 730, 735-739, 766), substantive footnotes additionally quote the content of entries from other books of the Gniezno Consistory, concerning matters previously submitted to the vicars general. These entries, in accordance with the principles described above, are also provided with Polish-language summaries. Indices placed after the edition form an integral part of the publication. The index of personal and place names covers all personal names, allows information on persons and objects to be found quickly, and additionally identifies all localities. The word index is a glossary of nouns and selected verbs relating to persons and functions, law, or material culture. The index is rounded off with a list of Polish terms containing information on more than 60 words and the contexts in which they are used, and a chronological concordance of entries from the individual parts of the individual parts of the critical edition.
